Key product: Complete tax-exemption application package.

7. Establish State and Local Compliance
Nonprofits may have obligations beyond incorporation and federal tax exemption.

Potential requirements include:

State tax-exemption filings

Charitable-solicitation registration

Annual corporate reports

Annual charitable-registration renewals

Local business licenses

Payroll registrations

Employment-related filings

Event permits

Regulated-service permits

Insurance requirements

Sales-tax exemption applications

Online fundraising can create multi-state compliance concerns because donors may be located outside the organization’s home state. Confirm requirements before broad public fundraising.

Key product: Compliance calendar listing each filing, deadline, fee, owner, and renewal date.

Part III — Financial Management and Accountability
8. Build the Financial Foundation
Strong financial management protects the organization’s mission, donors, beneficiaries, and board members.

Establish:

A bank account separate from all personal accounts.

Accounting software or a qualified bookkeeping process.

A nonprofit chart of accounts.

Annual operating budget.

Cash-flow forecast.

Donation and grant tracking.

Restricted-fund tracking.

Expense approval rules.

Receipt and invoice retention procedures.

Monthly bank reconciliations.

Monthly board financial reports.

Segregation of financial duties when practical.

Annual financial review process.

Never combine personal and organizational funds.

Key product: Budget, accounting system, financial controls policy, and monthly financial reporting package.

9. Plan Reporting, Tax Filings, and Donor Acknowledgments
Most tax-exempt organizations must submit an annual IRS information return or electronic notice. Organizations that fail to file required Form 990-series returns or notices for three consecutive years generally have their federal tax-exempt status automatically revoked. 
