Part IV — Programs, Fundraising, and Operations
10. Design Programs Around Measurable Outcomes
Programs should be designed around the change they are intended to create.

For each program, define:

The intended beneficiaries

Eligibility requirements

Services or activities provided

Program schedule

Staffing and volunteer requirements

Cost per participant or activity

Outputs, such as people served or supplies distributed

Outcomes, such as improved access, knowledge, stability, safety, or well-being

Data collection methods

Participant feedback process

Risks and safeguards

Collect only the personal information needed for service delivery, safety, reporting, or evaluation.

Key product: Program logic model and measurement plan.

11. Develop a Sustainable Fundraising Model
Do not depend solely on one fundraiser, one grant, one donor, or one event.

Potential funding sources include:

Individual donors

Monthly donors

Major gifts

Foundation grants

Corporate sponsorships

Workplace giving

Fundraising events

Peer-to-peer fundraising

Government grants or contracts

In-kind gifts

Mission-aligned earned revenue

For every revenue source, identify:

Expected amount

Timing

Cost to obtain the funds

Restrictions

Reporting obligations

Responsible person

Renewal likelihood

Risk if funding ends

For online giving, clearly explain how donations are processed, whether platform fees apply, and who provides donor receipts. 
ftc.gov

Key product: Twelve-month fundraising plan and donor stewardship process.

12. Establish Operational Systems
Build systems that allow the organization to function beyond its founder.

Create processes for:

Staff and volunteer roles

Volunteer screening and onboarding

Training

Scheduling

Program delivery

Incident reporting

Purchasing and vendor management

Contracts

Inventory

Events

Safeguarding

Participant communications

Records management

Emergency response

Continuity of operations

Key product: Operations manual and onboarding checklists.

Part V — Technology, Website, and Digital Presence
13. Build the Website as an Organizational Asset
A nonprofit website should support trust, fundraising, communication, service delivery, and accountability.

Initial website pages should include:

Home

About

Mission, vision, and values

Programs or services

Get help or access services

Donate

Volunteer

Partner or sponsor

Leadership and board

Contact

News or impact stories

Privacy policy

Financial transparency information

Accessibility information where appropriate

Before building, define:

Domain name

Hosting plan

Content management approach

Front-end technology

Back-end technology

Database requirements

Donation processor

Email platform

Donor or constituent relationship management system

User roles and access controls

Backup process

Update process

Ownership of accounts and source code

Key product: Website requirements document, sitemap, technology plan, and launch checklist.

14. Protect Website and Donor Data
At minimum, establish:

HTTPS for the public website

Strong, unique passwords

Multi-factor authentication where available

Regular software updates

Backups

Limited administrator access

Secure handling of payment information

Privacy notices

Account recovery procedures

Vendor access controls

Incident-response procedures

CISA recommends using HTTPS and maintaining timely remediation of critical and high vulnerabilities for internet-facing systems. 
