15. Build a Social Media and Communications Strategy
Use only the platforms that match your audiences and capacity.

Potential channels include:

Website

Email newsletter

Facebook

Instagram

LinkedIn

YouTube

TikTok

Community newsletters

Local media

Partner communications

Create:

Content calendar

Brand voice guide

Posting approval process

Participant photo and media-consent process

Crisis communications procedure

Account ownership register

Response standards for comments and messages

Credential and account-recovery procedures

Prioritize an email list because it is a communications channel the organization controls directly.

Key product: Communications plan and social media policy.

Part VI — Risk, Improvement, and Growth
16. Manage Risk and Obtain Appropriate Insurance
Common nonprofit risks include:

Financial fraud

Data breach

Volunteer or employee misconduct

Participant injury

Event liability

Safeguarding failures

Reputational harm

Conflicts of interest

Employment disputes

Loss of records

Loss of key personnel

Overreliance on one funder or technology vendor

Potential insurance needs may include general liability, directors and officers liability, cyber liability, professional liability, workers’ compensation, event coverage, vehicle coverage, and property insurance.

Key product: Risk register, insurance review, and incident-response plan.

17. Launch Small and Improve Continuously
Start with a manageable pilot.

Deliver one or two core programs.

Serve a realistic number of participants.

Track costs, outcomes, and feedback.

Identify operational bottlenecks.

Improve policies and systems.

Build credible evidence of impact.

Scale only after demonstrating a sustainable model.

Key product: Pilot report, lessons learned, and year-two improvement plan.

18. Build Long-Term Trust
Trust is central to nonprofit sustainability.

Demonstrate accountability by:

Communicating honestly.

Using donations as represented.

Maintaining accurate records.

Protecting donor and participant privacy.

Publishing appropriate financial and impact information.

Maintaining active board oversight.

Reporting both successes and lessons learned.

Responding professionally to concerns.

Avoiding exaggerated impact claims.

Key product: Annual impact report and website transparency page.

First-Year Milestones
Months 1–2: Validate the Concept
Research the community need.

Draft mission, vision, and initial program model.

Identify existing organizations.

Recruit prospective board members.

Decide whether to form independently, partner, or seek fiscal sponsorship.

Months 3–4: Establish the Organization
Form the entity.

Obtain an EIN.

Adopt bylaws and initial policies.

Hold first board meeting.

Open bank account.

Set up bookkeeping.

Months 5–6: Build Compliance and Funding Readiness
Prepare tax-exemption application if appropriate.

Complete necessary state registrations.

Build budget and financial controls.

Develop donation procedures.

Create fundraising plan.

Review insurance needs.

Months 7–9: Launch Programs and Digital Presence
Pilot core program.

Launch minimum viable website.

Establish online donation capability.

Create email and social media channels.

Begin collecting program data.

Months 10–12: Evaluate and Strengthen
Review finances and governance.

Evaluate program performance.

Update policies and systems.

Prepare required filings.

Build next-year budget.

Publish initial impact update.

Establish year-two goals.

